<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Arrange the Chairs. – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Arrange the Chairs.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give simple directions within the school. Interactive: Participate in short conversations about familiar topics.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give simple directions to find places in the school (e.g., "The cleaning area is next to the bathroom."). Can participate in short conversations about familiar tasks (e.g., "What are you doing?" "I am arranging the desks."). Can ask and answer questions about their favorite activities (e.g., "Can you help me clean the classroom, please?" "Yes, I can clean up the board.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a messy classroom with chairs and desks out of place, a dirty whiteboard, and a full trash can. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "Is this classroom neat or messy?" to elicit adjectives "messy" and "dirty". Then, the teacher shows a picture of students cleaning and arranging things. The teacher asks, "What are they doing?" to connect to the theme "Let's Arrange the Chairs." and review vocabulary from previous lessons (e.g., sweep, wipe, tidy).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using present continuous and imperatives related to the pictures. For example, pointing to a picture: "They are sweeping the floor." "Look, she is wiping the window." "Let's arrange the chairs!" The teacher clearly pronounces /r/ in "arrange" and /s/ in "supplies" while demonstrating actions. The teacher uses simple gestures and realia (e.g., pointing to actual chairs, a trash can, a whiteboard) to reinforce meaning.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with clear questions. For example:
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the classroom is 'messy,' is it clean or dirty?" (Expected answer: Dirty.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Arrange the chairs,' do I want them neat or all over the place?" (Expected answer: Neat/Organized.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue presented on a poster or projected, depicting two students (e.g., Leo and Ana) cleaning the classroom. The dialogue includes target grammar (present continuous, imperatives, modal "can") and vocabulary from the theme. The register is informal, suitable for 5th graders.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
          <b>Leo:</b> Ana, what are you doing?<br>
          <b>Ana:</b> I am wiping the desks. They are very dirty.<br>
          <b>Leo:</b> Oh, yes! Can I help you clean up the classroom?<br>
          <b>Ana:</b> Yes, please! I can arrange the chairs. Can you sweep the floor, please?<br>
          <b>Leo:</b> Yes, I can sweep quickly. And after that, let's put away the cleaning supplies carefully.<br>
          <b>Ana:</b> Great idea! Let's make our classroom tidy.
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and use basic expressions and simple phrases related to immediate needs and familiar topics, particularly when requesting and offering help within the classroom context.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Leo and Ana talking about?" (Expected answer: Cleaning the classroom.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b>
              <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Ana is sweeping the floor." (False)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can help clean up." (True)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They want a messy classroom." (False)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice giving simple directions using imperatives ("Sweep the floor, please."), offering help ("Can I help you wipe the desks?"), asking for help ("Can you arrange the chairs?"), and responding ("Yes, I can. I am sweeping."). Focus on correct use of present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can," along with politeness markers ("please").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Dialogue Completion.</b> The teacher distributes handouts with the dialogue from Stage 2, but with key verbs (sweep, wipe, arrange, clean up, put away) or modal verbs ("can") missing. Students work individually or in pairs to fill in the blanks, reading the completed dialogue aloud to a partner. The teacher monitors and corrects pronunciation, especially /r/ and /s/.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Picture Card Questions and Answers.</b> The teacher shows flashcards depicting various classroom cleaning actions (e.g., a student sweeping, another wiping a whiteboard, someone arranging chairs, putting away books). For each card, students ask and answer in pairs: "What is she/he doing?" (Expected answer: "She/He is sweeping the floor.") and "Can you [action]?" (Expected answer: "Yes, I can." or "No, I can't."). The teacher guides students to add "please" when asking, fostering sociolinguistic awareness.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Classroom Manager" Game.</b> Students work in pairs. One student is the "Classroom Manager" and gives instructions using imperatives and "please" (e.g., "Arrange the desks, please." "Wipe the window quickly."). The other student responds, "Yes, I can! I am [action]-ing the [object]." or "Sorry, I can't. I am [another action]-ing." Students switch roles after a few instructions.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play: "Our Tidy Classroom." Students work in groups of three. Each group receives a "Classroom Cleaning Task Card" with 3-4 specific cleaning items or actions (e.g., "Arrange the chairs," "Wipe the whiteboard," "Empty the trash can," "Sweep the floor").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students imagine their classroom is messy and they need to clean it together.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within each group, one student starts by suggesting a task or asking for help: "Let's arrange the chairs, please!" or "Can you help me wipe the table?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student responds by offering help or giving an instruction: "Yes, I can arrange the chairs carefully." or "Please sweep the floor, I am wiping the window."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can" to assign and accept tasks, offering and requesting help, and maintaining politeness with "please."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to use as much vocabulary from the list as possible (e.g., desk, table, whiteboard, trash can, dustpan, mop, sponge, bucket, cloth, cleaner, cleaning supplies; tidy, clean, organized, messy, neat, dirty; quickly, carefully, quietly).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group performs their short role-play for the class or for another group.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Tidy Classroom" role-play.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group role-play, interacting to clean the classroom.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target words and phrases clearly, including /r/ in "arrange" and /s/ in "supplies."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students effectively use at least 5-7 vocabulary words related to cleaning and classroom objects.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Students use present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can" appropriately to give instructions, offer/ask for help, and respond.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and fluency:</b> Students speak in short, simple sentences that are understandable, with few pauses.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and politeness:</b> Students engage with group members and use "please" when giving instructions or asking for help.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I could say: 'Can you help me...?'" (Yes/A little/No)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I used new cleaning words like..." (Students write 2 words)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when talking about cleaning our classroom?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and what can I practice more at home?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students actively participated in the speaking tasks, especially during the role-play. The "Classroom Manager" game was effective for practicing imperatives and responses. Some students still need more practice with the present continuous form (e.g., confusing "I sweep" with "I am sweeping") and consistently using "please." The pronunciation of /r/ and /s/ was generally good, but some still struggle with the /r/ sound in words like "arrange." I need to incorporate more structured speaking drills for present continuous in the next lesson's warm-up and ensure clearer modeling of polite requests.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will apply the vocabulary and grammatical structures (imperatives, present continuous, 'can') by writing simple instructions or short paragraphs about how they help clean their classroom or home, building on the oral practice from this lesson.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>